TITLE: Funding

OPPORTUNITY TEXT:
A budget of €10.2M (2022) Support from the French Public Authorities The French Ministry in charge of research has supported IHES since 1964. Today, the Ministry for Higher Education and Research awards the Institute an annual grant, representing about a third of its budget. As part of the Choose France for Science program, Zhongkai Tao, Assistant Professor of Mathematics, receives funding from the French government, administered by the National Research Agency (ANR) under the France 2030 initiative, reference “ANRS-25-CFFS-0012”. Four permanent researchers are affected to the French National Center for Scientific Research (CNRS), which pays for their salaries and funds a number of scientific events every year. Other institutional partners, such as Paris-Saclay University, the Jacques Hadamard Mathematical Foundation (FMJH), the CARMIN project and the French Alternative Energies and Atomic Energy Comission contribute to funding part of the Institute’s scientific activity. Funding from the European Research Council (ERC) Current projects funded by the European Research Council are: – FourIntExP (Fourier interpolation and universal optimality of the A2 lattice): led by Principal Investigator Danylo Radchenko, this project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon Europe research and innovation program, under grant agreement #101078782. – GravitaS (The gravitational S-matrix: from theory to experiment): led by Principal Investigator Julio Parra Martinez, this project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon Europe research and innovation program, under grant agreement #101221094. An international network This network was created in the 1970s, when the Institute started to broaden its funding sources by looking abroad: The Swiss Academy of Sciences (since 1973) Max-Planck Gesellschaft, Germany (since 1975) The Belgian Science Policy Office (since 1976) Gifts and donations In the late 1990s, under Jean-Pierre Bourguignon’s directorship, IHES set up a department dedicated to fundraising and an endowment fund. Gifts, donations, and asset revenues from the endowment fund now make up for around 34% of its operations. Make a gift Funding from the Simons Foundation The Simons Foundation is the institute’s most important donor. For twenty years, from the beginning of the fundraising activities undertaken by IHES in the 2000s, and until 2025, the Simons Foundation gave more than €25m to IHES. In 2021, James and Marilyn Simons, the co-founders of the Simons Foundation, pledged another 25m€ to support IHES over ten years: this is a record gift both for the Institute and for fundamental research in France. Depending on the Institute’s needs, these funds will be used to finance doctoral and post-doctoral fellowships, as well as one or more prestigious position(s) for a talented young scientist with an outstanding track record, as well as allow leading researchers to spend long sabbaticals at the Institute. This grant will also allow IHES to continue to develop initiatives to promote diversity and inclusion in mathematics and theoretical physics, by raising awareness of these issues within the scientific and mathematical communities as well as at the Institute itself. It will also contribute to the financing of projects that promote the dissemination of science and knowledge to the general public. A significant portion of the donation, given more specifically by the Simons Foundation International, will also strengthen the Institute’s endowment to ensure its financial independence and stability over the long-term. The Simons Foundation also funds specific projects, the Simons Collaborations, carried out by some of its permanent professors: Simons Collaboration on the Nonperturbative Bootstrap, led at IHES by Slava Rychkov; Simons Collaboration on Perfection in Algebra, Geometry, and Topology whose principal investigator at IHES is Dustin Clausen;

TITLE: AU-EU Youth Action Lab Grants 2026: Funding for Youth-Led Initiatives in Africa and Europe

OPPORTUNITY TEXT:
The AU-EU Youth Action Lab is launching its final Call for Proposals for youth-led initiatives seeking financial support to address global challenges in their communities. The programme supports young people and youth initiatives across Africa and Europe, providing funding designed to help turn innovative ideas, solutions, and advocacy efforts into practical action. For the final cohort, eligible youth initiatives can apply for one of two grant opportunities: Innovation Grants or Entrepreneurship Grants. Both grant types are designed around a “powershifting” approach that places young people at the centre of decisions about the initiatives intended to benefit them. The grants will support selected initiatives for 12 months, from January 2027 to December 2027. Application deadline: 23 September 2026 at 13:00 Central African Time (CAT). Important: Applicants can apply to only one of the two grant opportunities. About the AU-EU Youth Action Lab The AU-EU Youth Action Lab is focused on strengthening cooperation between young people in Africa and Europe. The programme creates opportunities for youth initiatives to connect with peers, exchange experiences, develop ideas, collaborate across contexts, and bring youth perspectives and solutions to decision-makers. The initiative recognises the role young people can play in addressing major challenges within their communities and beyond. Through its Youth Action Lab grants, the programme provides funding for youth-led initiatives that are developing solutions to global challenges or working to create more supportive environments for young people. The final Call for Proposals is specifically open to eligible youth initiatives in selected African countries. What Is the Purpose of the Youth Action Lab Grants? The grants are designed to help young people move from ideas and advocacy to practical implementation. The AU-EU Youth Action Lab is particularly interested in initiatives that can contribute to addressing global challenges within their own contexts. Rather than simply providing conventional project funding, the programme describes its grant approach as “powershifting.” This approach is built around three key principles: 1. Nothing About Youth Without Youth Young people should have a meaningful role in decisions concerning youth initiatives. To support this principle, a Youth Advisory Board of the Youth Action Lab will review and select proposals for funding. This means the grant-selection process is designed to give young people a direct role in determining which initiatives receive support. 2. Organisational Capacity The programme seeks to provide grants that do more than support individual activities. According to the AU-EU Youth Action Lab, the funding aims to facilitate and nurture: Organisational stability Resilience Financial security Organisational capacity This approach can help youth initiatives strengthen their ability to operate effectively and sustain their work. 3. Simplified Application and Reporting The Youth Action Lab also aims to make its application and reporting processes appropriate for youth initiatives. The programme emphasises application and reporting mechanisms that make sense for youth-led organisations and initiatives. This is intended to reduce unnecessary complexity and make the funding process more accessible to the initiatives the programme seeks to support. Two AU-EU Youth Action Lab Grant Opportunities For the final Call for Proposals, the Youth Action Lab is offering two different types of grants. Although each grant has a different focus, both are intended to support youth-led initiatives through the programme’s powershifting approach. 1. Innovation Grants The Innovation Grants are designed to support youth initiatives that want to design, test and/or scale innovations linked to global challenges. This grant category is particularly relevant to youth initiatives developing new approaches, ideas, models, tools, projects, or solutions to challenges affecting their communities. An initiative may be at a stage where it is: Designing a new solution; Testing an existing idea; Developing an innovative approach;

TITLE: 2027 Mandela Washington Fellowship for Young African Leaders – Fully Funded Leadership Opportunity in the United States

OPPORTUNITY TEXT:
The 2027 Mandela Washington Fellowship for Young African Leaders will open for applications on September 22, 2026, giving young African leaders an opportunity to participate in a prestigious leadership development and professional exchange program in the United States. The Fellowship is the flagship program of the U.S. Government’s Young African Leaders Initiative (YALI) and is designed for accomplished young leaders who are already making a difference in their communities, countries, and professional sectors. For the 2027 Fellowship, eligible applicants may apply to one of three tracks: Business and Trade Emerging Technologies Governance and Security The program brings together emerging African leaders for intensive learning, professional networking, leadership development, and engagement with American institutions and professionals. About the Mandela Washington Fellowship Established in 2014, the Mandela Washington Fellowship has developed a growing network of young African leaders from across Sub-Saharan Africa. The official Fellowship website reports that nearly 7,800 young leaders from all 49 countries in Sub-Saharan Africa have participated since the program began. The Fellowship is intended for accomplished young professionals and leaders with demonstrated experience in leadership, innovation, public service, business, civic engagement, or other areas of community impact. Rather than being a traditional academic degree program, the Fellowship combines leadership development, practical learning, professional networking, and cultural exchange. 2027 Fellowship Tracks Applicants for the 2027 program will be able to apply to one of three specialized tracks. 1. Business and Trade The Business and Trade track is designed for leaders working in areas connected to entrepreneurship, business development, trade, investment, economic growth, and related fields. Participants can use the Fellowship to develop practical knowledge, exchange ideas with peers, and build professional relationships with organizations and leaders in the United States. 2. Emerging Technologies The Emerging Technologies track focuses on leaders working in technology and emerging areas shaping economies and societies. The track provides an opportunity for participants to explore innovative approaches, strengthen professional skills, and connect with others working at the intersection of technology, leadership, and development. 3. Governance and Security The Governance and Security track is intended for leaders working in areas related to governance, public service, security, policy, and related fields. Participants have opportunities to exchange perspectives with other African leaders and engage with U.S. institutions and professionals. What Does the Fellowship Include? The Mandela Washington Fellowship includes an intensive six-week Leadership Institute at a U.S. college or university. Fellows participate in academic and practical activities while sharing experiences and perspectives with their peers and U.S. communities. Depending on the Fellowship cycle and participant selection, program activities can include: Leadership and professional development Academic and practical learning Networking with other African leaders Engagement with U.S. businesses, organizations, and professionals Community and cultural activities Opportunities to exchange ideas and experiences across sectors Continued engagement through the Fellowship’s alumni network The broader Fellowship also includes professional and reciprocal exchange opportunities designed to strengthen relationships between African and U.S. participants. Who Should Apply? The Mandela Washington Fellowship is aimed at young African leaders aged 25 to 35 who have established records of leadership and accomplishment. The official Fellowship describes its participants as accomplished innovators and leaders who have demonstrated positive impact in their communities and countries. Previous Fellowship eligibility requirements have included applicants who: Are between 25 and 35 years old at the time specified by the application cycle, although exceptional applicants aged 21–24 may be considered; Are citizens and residents of an eligible Sub-Saharan African country; Are proficient in English; Are not U.S. citizens or permanent residents; Are eligible to receive a U.S. J-1 visa; Are not employees or immediate family members of employees of the U.S. Government;

TITLE: Innovative Clip-off Chemistry Enables Fast and Precise Production of Complex Molecules

OPPORTUNITY TEXT:
Researchers at the BIST Community centre ICN2 have developed a novel strategy to obtain complex organic molecules, known as macrocycles, by precisely breaking down crystalline structures. This method enables target molecules to be ‘cut out’ rather than built up through traditional synthesis. The study has been published in Science. A team of ICN2 scientists has led the development of a pioneering method for obtaining various types of organic molecule through a process of molecular ‘cutting’. This approach allows the rapid and precise isolation of target molecules, avoiding the slower and more complicated procedures usually associated with traditional chemical synthesis. These results pave the way for the easier and more efficient production of complex molecules, with promising applications in areas such as the development of new materials. Specifically, the study introduces a method of extracting macrocycles, which are cyclic organic molecules that are already used in several industries, such as food, cosmetics, and drug delivery. To obtain them, the team started with larger porous crystalline structures called COFs (covalent organic frameworks), which contained the desired macrocycles within their molecular framework. COFs are widely studied molecules due to their potential applications in areas such as gas storage. The work, published in the journal Science, is the result of an international collaboration involving researchers from several institutions, including the Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona (UAB), the University of Girona, the University of California Berkeley, and the Institute of Materials Science of Barcelona (ICMAB-CSIC). Clip-off Chemistry: The Art of “Cutting Out” Molecules Dr. Daniel Maspoch, ICREA research prof. at ICN2. The technique is based on the concept of Clip-off Chemistry, developed under the leadership of ICREA Prof. Daniel Maspoch, head of the ICN2 Supramolecular NanoChemistry and Materials Group and lead author of the study. The work was also led by other members of his group, Drs. Inhar Imaz and Jorge Albalad. This strategy relies on materials that already incorporate the target molecules within their structure, which are then selectively “clipped off” and released. The COFs used in the study were designed in advance using simple molecular precursors, with specific chemical bonds incorporated at strategic positions, such as double and triple bonds between carbon atoms which can be selectively broken. Once the COFs had been synthesised, the next step was to release the macrocycles. To achieve this, the researchers employed ozone gas as a molecular ‘scalpel’. This gas is made up of three oxygen atoms and can break these reactive bonds through a process known as ozonolysis. This releases the macrocycles quickly and efficiently, eliminating the need for slow and complex synthesis routes. As Prof. Maspoch explains, “We design materials that already contain the rings we’re aiming for, using simple building blocks — like LEGO pieces — and then we release them with surgical precision”. Enormous Potential Across Fields Using this strategy, the researchers successfully synthesised nine different types of macrocycle, some of which contained up to 162 atoms. These included several chemical structures and various functional groups, such as aldehydes and carboxylic acids, which demonstrates the technique’s versatility. The resulting macrocycles’ structure was confirmed using advanced techniques such as mass spectrometry and scanning tunnelling microscopy. Significant contributions to these analyses were made by the ICN2 Atomic Manipulation and Spectroscopy Group, led by ICREA Prof. Aitor Mugarza. According to the authors, “This method paves the way for a new and versatile approach to obtaining complex molecules. It has enormous potential for use in diverse fields such as organic chemistry, nanotechnology, and the development of new materials, devices, and biosensors.” Reference article: Sánchez-Naya, R; Cavalieri, JP; Albalad, J; Cortés-Martínez, A; Wang, K; Fuertes-Espinosa, C; r Parella, T;

TITLE: Procurement Consultant for ACE Impact

